Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.429 align:start position:0%
 
if you live in a relatively large  european city it's very likely that  you've seen a company like gatier flink  or gorillas  what these companies do is pretty simple  deliver you your groceries very quickly  but if you're paying a bit more  attention you might also ask yourself  how they've managed to be practically  everywhere  i see fast grocery delivery writers  every single time i go outside and i see  their marketing on practically  everything  even gorillas recognizable for their  pitch black outfits didn't exist one and  a half years ago and it has already  become europe's fastest growing unicorn  ever surpassing a valuation of one  billion dollars  the rise of these companies begs two  simple questions why are they so  valuable and why is europe particularly  dominant in producing them  you an average consumer lazy that you  are face multiple costs beyond the  sticker price you see while buying  groceries if i wanted to go to the  grocery store right now for example i  would have to grab my keys from the  shelf at the other end of the room lock  my door walk to the store make painful  eye contact with people on the street  look for my food in the store and then i  have to walk back home again make eye  contact with people on the street again  unlock my doors put my keys back on the  shelf and then prepare a meal all of  these actions require something for me  so it's understandable how these fast  grocery delivery companies have managed  to make a somewhat sustainable business  by promising me to cut these extra costs  out these companies know that we'll  practically always have our phones on us  so instead of facing the travel costs  you would normally need to experience  all you need to do instead is open up  their app type the basic consumer good  you want in the search bar face a more  than manageable markup the cost they add  up as a company beyond the sticker price  they initially paid for their goods and  then wait for it to arrive in many cases  the food will arrive faster than what it  would have taken for you to get it  yourself the value proposition these  companies have makes sense but it's much  less clear if their extreme valuations  do  get the turkey-based company that's  currently leading the pack has a 7.5  billion dollar valuation and closed  around 1.1 billion dollars in  fundraising guerrillas the german-based  company has received outside funding  from investors like the chinese gaming  giant tencent and also has a billion  dollar valuation flink the third largest  has raised hundreds of millions of euros  in funds 240 million to be exact and  multiple other delivery startups that i  haven't even mentioned have also  received millions  the fact that these companies have seen  massive inflows of capital pretty much  explains the entire story there's  practically no way that there could be  so many of these fast delivery companies  in the same city that are offering their  products for only a fraction more than  the regular prices in the supermarket  while also burning a ton of capital on  promotion and marketing the only reason  they can offer such a cheap service  while excessively advertising is because  they're all insulated by large amounts  of venture capital funding if i walk  around my city i'll have a near  guarantee that i'll see a tram covered  with couture branding and i'll see  plenty of bikers with the corresponding  attire with all different but  recognizable colors  the exposure to the excessive branding  reveals a deeper level of competition  under the water in order to crowd the  others out you're going to see the use  of any mechanism that can attract the  most consumers fastest pushing their  laborers to the limit downward pressure  on the price that they charge and a  serious amount of marketing and  promotion  the reason the marketing and promotion  is so important is because the main  products these companies provide  alongside their delivery services are  relatively homogeneous with little to no  room for differentiation  it's quite unlikely that people will  have multiple fast grocery delivery apps  when each delivery app provides  essentially the same not really  differentiable goods a creative heineken  some dish soap or ben and jerry's ice  cream when it's convenient  what the companies really want is for  their app to be the first one you  actually download  and venture capitalists know that it's a  matter of betting on the right horse  that can weather the storm with the  storm being the other delivery companies  so they'll load up the brands with  enough funding to the point that the  individual company can still operate at  a relative loss while the others  inevitably lose  gorillas has already been compared with  wework the poster child for an  overvalued startup in the us they face a  lot of criticism for pushing their gig  workers extremely hard and they've also  received hundreds of millions in funding  the high amount of flowing capital has  been partially attributed to the  presence of gorilla's charismatic ceo  who seems to be religiously concerned  with spending money to scale before  getting the revenue stream to match  guerrillas predicted an annual  reoccurring revenue of 2.6 billion  dollars by the end of 2021 however in  april of this year its annual recurring  revenue was only 196 million a steep  uphill climb is necessary to meet their  own self-laid expectations  we can't see their consistent revenue  figures right now because they're a  private company but i'm willing to bet  that they're still pretty shy of 2.6  billion dollars in revenue  it's not to say that these companies  make miscalculations on purpose or that  gorillas couldn't meet that revenue  eventually but it shows that the  priorities of these companies are scale  scale and against scale before actual  predictable revenue is produced  guitier gorillas and flink are all  european-based companies although the us  may still have unicorns that are just as  valuable or even more valuable the speed  at which these european firms have grown  is particularly relevant  although a lot of americans like to  think that the reason they have more  globally successful companies is due to  a bunch of individual factors most  europeans know the giant elephant in the  room the u.s simply has a larger market  it's not so much a question of more or  less inherent skill as much as it is a  question of a more or less unified  consumer base which the us has while  europe does not  so when european food tech companies  grow faster than their american  counterparts that requires a bit more  scrutiny  an obvious reason for why european food  tech unicorns can grow so fast is  because of the playing field they're  operating in compared to other places  europe is simply ahead with public  infrastructure like bike lanes that  provide ample more affordable  opportunities in building a delivery  force  this stands in clear contrast with  delivery services that are primarily  reliant on cars for deliveries  where cities in america may currently  have difficulty adopting cargo bikes  it's already the norm in many european  cities  it's safe to say that the playing field  for fast grocery delivery companies will  thin eventually so treat the app you  install as almost a vote for which one  you actually want to survive it's  impressive how much these companies have  grown but it's also not entirely a  surprise the venture capital funding  landscape of europe is growing  tremendously and the rise of these  european giants is a reflection of a  development we may have to get used to  in the future  [Music]  foreign

